A||||12||billfredmike24||Coolant recovery tank overflow||||1||
Z||000000||billfredmike24||09-16-2001||12:00 AM||||Just got insurance put on my 85gt yesterday so i took it for a spin today for about half an hour.  The car ran fine the whole time, temp. gauge never went over half, parked the the car in the garage, turned it off, and opened front and back.  Everthing seemed fine until the coolant recovery tank kept filling, and filling, then overflowed for about a minute, went to get some towels to mop it up, came back and it was back down to just a couple inches in the bottom of the tank.  Took it for another spin and the temp gauge was hot then cold, hot then cold.  But no overflow this time, nothin came back in the tank.<BR>What do i do now, add more coolant?, i checked the rad and it was full.  Any suggestions.<BR>TIA Mike  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigt_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||afRaceR||09-16-2001||12:04 AM||||Goto the parts store, get a non-vented radiator cap. Open up the engine coolant cap and fill it up there. <BR>Parts books list the wrong radiator cap for the fiero, the list a vented cap.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rick 88||09-16-2001||12:05 AM||||How old is the thermostat? Sounds like it is sticking.<P>Rick 88||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||09-16-2001||12:10 AM||||replace the thermostat, the thermostat cap, and the radiator cap. read my coolant fill article. It explains the filling and the differences between the caps available.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||09-16-2001||12:22 AM||||Yes, do what these guys said.<P>O/T, do you have any other pics of your fiero? Silver looks like a nice color for my 86 SE  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I like the wheels too.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">         <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC (FOR SALE)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tina||09-16-2001||11:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by billfredmike24:<BR><B><BR>What do i do now<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Whatever you end up doing, treat that overflow tank like raw eggs.<BR>(Plastic on upper left corner get brittle over time. no replacements available either)<BR>Doesn't take much to crack it.<P>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||billfredmike24||09-16-2001||01:18 PM||||Thanks for the info. all.<P>Sqoach check out "my new fiero" in genereal section, within the last month.<BR>Thanks Mike||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||09-16-2001||01:26 PM||||Most likly your rad cap is the problem. I had the same problem with mine a few months back. I however used a vented cap and have had absolutly no problems with it since I put it on 4-5 months ago, so I see no problems with useing it, as long as you park on a fairly level surface most of the time.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 4spd Auto, getting ready to install T-6 turbo.||||reg||1
Z||000008||8Ball||09-16-2001||02:23 PM||||I have been haveing a similar problem And am about to replace the caps, the thermostat, and have replaced the busted water pump. But I have one more complication. Something is dripping on my foot at the gas pedal? Is this the heater core? cause I thought it was on the passenger side. Or is it condensation where my overflow cottle is boiling over?<P>Randy<P>P.S. I will not be able to make it to the run for the hills as I am still haveing cooling system problems||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-16-2001||03:45 PM||||dripping on your foot could be a holed  heater or the AC drian blocked. A holed heater would let air into the system as well as let water out... That could cause water to run into the overflow, especially with the vented cap.<P>While the vented cap can work just fine, I really recomend the nonvented caps. Then if you do have to park on a slope or something you won't have to worry about it. The cooling system really won't care which one you use.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||09-16-2001||04:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by billfredmike24:<BR><B>Thanks for the info. all.<P>Sqoach check out "my new fiero" in genereal section, within the last month.<BR>Thanks Mike</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh yeah. I even replied to that post  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I didn't get to see it after the wheels were put on, though. At first I thought you had put t-tops on, but noticed that you replaced the roof with your parts car's black roof. Looks pretty neat like that. Looking good so far  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||HummelHund||09-16-2001||04:46 PM||||I changed the radiator cap (to a non-vented), the thermostat, and the thermo housing cap, and my 84' SE still spat up coolant. The local Fiero guru suggested sanding or filing down the rough spots on the top of the thermo housing, he said if this was uneven that it could let air in and make the coolant overflow. Sure enough, my thermo housing was sort of bumpy ffeling, so I filed it down until it was flat all the way around the lip. I put on a new thermo housing cap and haven't had a single overflow problem since. Anyone else used this method, did it work for you?<P><BR>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project/daily driver)<BR>'84 SE (Dad's Project)<p>[This message has been edited by HummelHund (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||09-16-2001||06:08 PM||||rough area on the thermo neck can do exactly what you found. That's definatly one thing to check.||||reg||1
